## The temple you arrive at by water.

There are temples you drive up to, and there is Philae. You leave the car at the water, step into a boat of your own, and cross a lake of granite islands with the engine low. Then the kiosk of Trajan comes into view, standing at the water's edge on its fourteen columns, and behind it the great pylon of the Temple of Isis. Very few places in Egypt give you that kind of arrival.

You come at a quiet hour. The stone is still cool, and the colonnades throw long shadows across the forecourt, with room to take it in.

## The goddess who stayed the longest

Isis was the great mother of Egyptian belief: the wife who gathered Osiris back together, the mother who hid and protected Horus, and the one people prayed to for healing and safe return. Her worship spread across the Roman world, and Philae was its heart. Long after the other temples of Egypt had fallen silent, pilgrims still came here, and the temple kept its rites until the sixth century AD.

You see it in the walls. The pylons show the kings who built and rebuilt the temple, offering to Isis and to Horus, figures cut deep enough to hold shadow even at midday. Inside, the courts narrow towards the sanctuary where her image once stood.

> Here, a goddess outlasted an empire. You can feel that persistence in the stone.

## A temple rescued from the water

The island you walk on is not the island Isis knew. When the first Aswan dam was raised, Philae spent much of every year half under water, and visitors rowed between the capitals of drowned columns. When the High Dam threatened to submerge it for good, an international rescue took the whole temple apart, block by block, and rebuilt it on higher ground on Agilkia Island, completed in 1980. Standing in the forecourt, you would never guess.

## Left to us

The tickets, the boat, the hour: your concierge takes care of all of it, with local knowledge and our craft. You simply arrive, and have time to sit in the shade of the colonnade rather than be hurried to the next stop. Afterwards the day carries on as you like: Elephantine, the Nubian Museum, or simply a long lunch on the river.
